## Why This Is Asked
Probes real-world SRE orchestration for multi-tenant systems under burst traffic, requiring dynamic budgets, fairness, and controlled degradation.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant SLOs and budgets
- Fusion of signals: latency, error rate, queue depth
- Admission control and load shedding
- Safe rollback plan with tenant-level validation
- Observability and audit logging

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code for tenant budget enforcement
function allocate(tenantId, req) {
  const budget = getBudget(tenantId);
  if (budget.remaining <= 0) return reject(req, "budget exhausted");
  // consume
  budget.consume(1);
  return forward(req);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate budget fairness under mixed workloads?
- How would you test the load-shedding policy without impacting real tenants?

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes practical incident engineering: how to fuse disparate signals, assign accountability, and make safe change decisions under pressure.

## Key Concepts

- Multi-signal correlation across metrics/logs/traces
- SLO-aligned alert routing and ownership mapping
- Canary and synthetic testing for regression prevention
- RCA templating and rollback playbooks

## Code Example

```javascript
// Simple rule mapping alerts to on-call owners
const owners = {
  payments: "team-payments",
  auth: "team-auth",
  inventory: "team-inventory"
};
function route(alert) {
  const svc = alert.tags?.service || "general";
  return owners[svc] || "oncall";
}
```

